The specification below counts the digit 7 directly from the docstring. Because n <= 300, every i is below 300 and has at most three digits. So it reads the units, tens and hundreds digits arithmetically instead of using a digit loop, which keeps the work small for the verifier. Leading-zero digits never equal 7, so they don't affect the count. For i = 0, which is divisible by both 11 and 13, the representation "0" contains no 7.

```python
def pre(n):
    return 0 <= n <= 300

def _sevens_in(i):
    # i is in [0, 299], so it has at most three decimal digits.
    c = 0
    if i % 10 == 7:
        c += 1
    if (i // 10) % 10 == 7:
        c += 1
    if (i // 100) % 10 == 7:
        c += 1
    return c

def post(n, result):
    expected = 0
    for i in range(n):
        if i % 11 == 0 or i % 13 == 0:
            expected += _sevens_in(i)
    return result == expected
```
